{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1785688416,"nonce":"gLxTxHIM8jVJftkaAFOk6ZLyaio=","title":"Measured 2026-08-02, Apple M5, release build, real `workspaced::router` + real `TieredCas` over loopback HTTP driven by the real `WorkspaceClient`; feed leg is `Cas::materialize` — the exact call `scarab-wsfetch fetch` makes. 8.19 MB constant at 250 and 2000 files, uniform sizes, distinct pseudorandom content per file, mean of 3. Benchmark lives at `crates/scarab-workspace-client/tests/feed_cost.rs` (`#[ignore]`d):","message":"cargo test --release -p scarab-workspace-client --test feed_cost -- --ignored --nocapture --test-threads=1\n\n| leg | files | today | ms/file | ADR-0061 s0 | then |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| feed `materialize` | 2000 | 1076 ms | **0.54** | 11581 ms | 5.79 |\n| feed `materialize` | 250 | 147 ms | **0.59** | 2300 ms | 9.20 |\n| drain `ingest` cold | 2000 | 5360 ms | **2.68** | 8867 ms | 4.43 |\n| drain `ingest` deduped | 2000 | 642 ms | 0.32 | — | — |\n| feed re-fetch, fresh dir | 2000 | 829 ms | 0.41 | — | — |\n| one `/flat` call | 2000 | 49 ms | 0.025 | — | — |\n\n**THE FEED IS NO LONGER THE BOTTLENECK — THE DRAIN IS.** At 50k files: feed ~27 s, cold drain ~134 s, deduped drain ~16 s. The drain is 5x the feed.\n\n**Composition of the feed's 1076 ms:** 49 ms `/flat` + 434 ms blob GETs + **~590 ms (55%) local filesystem writes and metadata restore**. The filesystem half is the larger half and a lazy mount still pays it on first read.\n\n**Corrections to ADR-0061 s0:**\n- Its finding 3 (\"dedup saves storage, not wall-clock\") is now **FALSE**: deduped re-ingest is 8.3x faster than cold (642 vs 5360 ms). The batched `/have` bought wall-clock.\n- Its finding 2 (cost tracks file count, not bytes) **still holds** — feed per-file cost is flat across an 8x file-count change (0.59 -\u003e 0.54), so linear extrapolation is defensible.\n\n**Incidental findings, both cheap to fix:**\n- `CONCURRENCY = 16` is a HARD-CODED const at `crates/scarab-workspace-client/src/lib.rs:64` — NOT `SCARAB_CAS_CONCURRENCY` (that is the Depot's own object-store legs, default 32).\n- **Tree PUTs are a serial `for` loop.** Blob GETs/PUTs are 16-wide.\n- A width sweep over 2000 GETs saturates at 4x (1-\u003e1800 ms, 4-\u003e434, 16-\u003e434, 128-\u003e431): CPU-bound on this harness, not RTT-bound — but client and Depot share 10 cores in ONE process here, so a real Pod may differ.\n\n**Bias, stated:** loopback TCP, warm tier on local disk, client+server in one process. Understates production (a real network adds an RTT per blob), but the amortised RTT at width \u003e=16 is small next to the 55% filesystem half, which a network does not change. Not measured: `widen_for_the_group`'s recursive chmod walk (~2 syscalls/file, estimated \u003c5%).\n\n**VERDICT: eliminating the feed copy does not justify a stateful NFS server on the critical path.** ~27 s on a 50k-file workspace, over half of it filesystem work laziness must pay anyway on first read.\n\n- [ ] Record this against ADR-0061 s0 (correcting finding 3) and ADR-0062 (2b marked not-justified-by-current-numbers, NOT rejected on its structural argument)\n- [ ] THE OPEN QUESTION THAT COULD REVIVE 2b: what fraction of a workspace does a real Step actually READ? 27 s is the CEILING on the laziness win; the real win is 27 s x read-fraction. Nobody has measured it. For `cargo build` the read fraction is probably high, which would make laziness worth much less than the ceiling — but that is a guess.\n- [ ] Re-target the effort at the DRAIN: terminal-workspace skip (removes it entirely for leaf steps), parallel tree PUTs, configurable client concurrency","files":null}]}